# Intentionally carries no fingerprints. Prefer the mechanisms below.
#
# Entries here are keyed on FILE:RULE:LINE, so any edit ABOVE a suppressed line
# silently voids the suppression, and nothing reports that it happened. That is
# not hypothetical. The entry for BRANCHES_test_ini.py was pinned to line 352 on
# 2026-03-12 (c44ec3e448); on 2026-07-30 commit d0e45ce33b netted one line out
# of that file above the fixture, moving the finding to 351. The suppression was
# dead for seven weeks, and its finding was reaching the release gate.
#
# Of the ten fingerprints this file used to hold, four were dead: that one, plus
# three .env entries superseded by the .env path allowlist in .gitleaks.toml.
# The other six were live and load-bearing. All ten have been retired in favour
# of a mechanism that cannot drift:
#
#   - `gitleaks:allow` ON the offending line, for a single deliberate
#     credential-shaped string in source (test fixtures, scrubber bait, a pinned
#     upstream commit SHA). It travels with the line, so it survives both edits
#     above it and changes to the value itself, and it blinds exactly that line
#     -- a real token added elsewhere in the same file still fires.
#
#   - a path allowlist in .gitleaks.toml, for a whole CLASS of non-source files
#     (agent runtime state, bytecode caches, generated caches whose filenames
#     rotate, the gitignored .env). Only for paths that are never publishable.
#
#   - a regex allowlist in .gitleaks.toml, for a fixed benign string in a file
#     that IS committed source. Prefer this over a path allowlist there: tracker
#     .ops files are committed, so allowlisting the path would blind the scanner
#     to a real credential pasted into a discussion thread.
#
# Better than any of them: delete the bait. A fixture value that no assertion
# reads does not need to look like a credential.
#
# Add a fingerprint here only when none of the above fits, and treat it as
# perishable: re-verify it whenever the file it points into changes. Note that
# `-i <other-path>` does NOT disable this file -- gitleaks still reads the one
# at the scan root -- so an "ignores off" control run needs a real control.
